In Spring Transaction Management, two important concepts are:

🔹 Propagation

It defines how a transaction boundary behaves when a method is called inside another transactional method.
Think of it like: If Method A is already running in a transaction, and it calls Method B, should B join the same transaction or start a new one?

Common Propagation Types

REQUIRED (default)

If a transaction exists, join it; else start a new one.

✅ Most commonly used.

@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRED)
public void methodA() {}


REQUIRES_NEW

Always starts a new transaction, suspends the current one.

Useful when one operation must be committed independently of the outer transaction.

SUPPORTS

If a transaction exists, join it; else run without one.

MANDATORY

Must run inside an existing transaction, else throw exception.

NOT_SUPPORTED

Run without a transaction, suspend the current one if exists.

NEVER

Must run without a transaction, else throw exception.

NESTED

Executes in a nested transaction inside the current one (like savepoints).

If nested fails, outer transaction can continue.

🔹 Isolation

It defines how data consistency is maintained when multiple transactions run at the same time.
In databases, transactions may conflict (e.g., reading uncommitted data, dirty reads).
Isolation controls that.

Isolation Levels

DEFAULT

Uses the DB’s default isolation level (varies: e.g., MySQL → REPEATABLE_READ).

READ_UNCOMMITTED

Lowest isolation, allows dirty reads.

One transaction can see uncommitted changes of another.

READ_COMMITTED

Prevents dirty reads (only committed data is visible).

Still allows non-repeatable reads & phantom reads.

REPEATABLE_READ

Prevents dirty reads + non-repeatable reads.

Phantom reads still possible (MySQL actually prevents those too with gap locks).

SERIALIZABLE

Highest isolation. Transactions are executed one by one.

Prevents all anomalies but reduces concurrency.

📝 Example in Spring
@Transactional(
    propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW,
    isolation = Isolation.REPEATABLE_READ
)
public void processPayment() {
    // business logic here
}


🔹 Key Problems with Uncommitted Data

Dirty Read → One transaction reads uncommitted data from another.

If the first transaction later rolls back, the second transaction has read "invalid" data.

✅ In short:

Committed data = permanent, safe, visible to everyone.

Uncommitted data = temporary, risky, visible only within the transaction (unless isolation is very low).



⚖️ Summary:

Propagation = how transaction behaves across method calls.

Isolation = how transaction ensures data consistency when multiple transactions run concurrently.